{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"magnetic quantity unit conversion magnetic quantity unit conversion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md","fileName":"Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Algebraic SI Derived Units","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"magnetic quantity unit conversion magnetic quantity unit conversion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md","fileName":"Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion","heading":"Magnetic Quantity Unit Conversion","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Magnetic field strength, magnetic flux, and magnetic flux density have different unit expressions and separate tabs in Pryor's converter. Its examples convert oersted to A/m, maxwell to nanoweber, and gauss to millitesla. The maxwell example passes through webers and a nano- prefix; the gauss example passes through teslas and a milli- prefix. Each chain preserves a different physical quantity, so the three conversion factors should not be mixed simply because all are magnetic. See [[Algebraic SI Derived Units]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering an